[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a complete, copy-and-paste-ready recipe for loading a **single `.pmtiles` archive** (vector, raster or raster-DEM) into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

MapLibre itself does not understand the `pmtiles://` protocol, but the **`pmtiles` npm package** ships a tiny protocol handler that does the range-request magic for you.

```bash
# From your project root
npm install pmtiles
# (or yarn add pmtiles, pnpm add pmtiles)
```

> **Why a library?**  
> The handler knows how to read the archive header, build a TileJSON on-the-fly, and return the raw tile bytes in the shape MapLibre expects (`{data: …}`).

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
// src/pmtiles-setup.js  (or any file that runs at app start)
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Create the protocol instance
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it under the scheme name "pmtiles"
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);

// Optional clean-up (e.g. in a React useEffect return)
// maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
```

> **Important:**  
> *Do this only once.* The protocol registry is global, so registering it a second time does nothing useful and can cause memory leaks in long-running apps.

> **MapLibre v4+ note** – `addProtocol` now expects an **async handler that resolves to `{data, cacheControl?, expires?}`**. The `pmtiles.Protocol().tile` method already contains a compatibility shim (`v3compat`) that works on v3, v4, v5 and v6, so you don’t have to write your own handler.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a source that points at the `.pmtiles` file

You have two choices for the URL format:

| Form | When to use | What MapLibre reads |
|------|-------------|---------------------|
| `url: "pmtiles://https://example.com/tiles.pmtiles"` | **Recommended** – lets the protocol read the archive header and automatically supply `minzoom`, `maxzoom`, `bounds`, etc. | TileJSON generated on-the-fly |
| `tiles: ["pmtiles://https://example.com/tiles.pmtiles/{z}/{x}/{y}"]` | Only if you *must* supply a template (rare). You lose the automatic zoom-range handling. | No TileJSON; you must set `minzoom`/`maxzoom` yourself |

### 3-a️⃣ Vector tiles (most common)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "myPmtiles": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "pmtiles://https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/world.pmtiles"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "water",
      "type": "fill",
      "source": "myPmtiles",
      "source-layer": "water",          // name of the layer inside the archive
      "paint": { "fill-color": "#a0c8f0" }
    },
    {
      "id": "roads",
      "type": "line",
      "source": "myPmtiles",
      "source-layer": "road",
      "paint": { "line-color": "#777", "line-width": 1 }
    }
    // …more layers that reference the same source but different source-layer names
  ]
}
```

### 3-b️⃣ Raster imagery

```json
{
  "sources": {
    "satellite": {
      "type": "raster",
      "url": "pmtiles://https://cdn.example.com/sat.pmtiles",
      "tileSize": 256               // optional, defaults to 256
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "sat",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "satellite"
    }
  ]
}
```

### 3-c️⃣ Raster-DEM (terrain)

```json
{
  "sources": {
    "terrain": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "url": "pmtiles://https://cdn.example.com/elev.pmtiles",
      "encoding": "terrarium"      // or "mapbox"
    }
  },
  "terrain": {
    "source": "terrain",
    "exaggeration": 1.5
  }
}
```

> **`source-layer`** – The name of the vector layer inside the archive. You can discover it with `pmtiles show <file>` or by opening the file in a viewer such as **PMTiles Viewer** (https://pmtiles.io/).

---

## 4️⃣ Create the MapLibre map

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import './pmtiles-setup.js';   // ensures the protocol is registered

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',            // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: { /* paste the JSON from step 3 */ },
    layers: [ /* paste the layers from step 3 */ ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

That’s it—MapLibre will now request **byte ranges** from the `.pmtiles` file, decode the tiles, and render them.

---

## 5️⃣ Hosting requirements (so the browser can fetch the file)

| Requirement | What to check |
|-------------|----------------|
| **Range requests** | The server must answer `Accept-Ranges: bytes` and honor `Range: bytes=start-end`. All major static hosts (AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, etc.) do this out of the box. |
| **CORS** | If the `.pmtiles` URL is on a different origin, the response must include `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) **and** `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range`. Most CDNs let you set these headers in the bucket or via a small edge-function. |
| **Cache-Control** | Because the file never changes, give it a long max-age (e.g. `public, max-age=31536000`). CDNs will cache the range responses as well. |
| **File size limits** | GitHub Pages caps static files at 100 MiB; other hosts usually allow many gigabytes. Split a huge dataset into multiple `.pmtiles` files if you hit a limit. |

---

## 6️⃣ Debugging checklist

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Blank vector tiles** (no features) | Using `tiles:` instead of `url:` → MapLibre thinks the archive has zoom levels it doesn’t. | Switch to `url:`; the protocol will read the header and over-zoom correctly. |
| **`Failed to fetch` / CORS error** | Host not sending `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` or `Accept-Ranges`. | Add the headers in your bucket/CDN configuration. |
| **`pmtilesProtocol.tile` returns empty data** | Using an old `pmtiles` (< 3.0) that only supports the callback API. | Upgrade to the latest `pmtiles` package (≥ 3.0). |
| **MapLibre logs “Protocol handler returned undefined”** | You wrote a custom handler that returns a raw `ArrayBuffer` instead of `{data: …}` (MapLibre v4+). | Wrap the buffer: `return {data: buffer}`. |
| **Tiles load but are mis-aligned** | The archive was generated with a non-standard tile scheme (e.g. TMS vs. XYZ). | Regenerate with `--xyz` (tippecanoe) or ensure the source uses the same scheme as MapLibre (XYZ). |

---

## 7️⃣ Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>PMTiles + MapLibre</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.2.0/+esm';

  // 1️⃣ Register protocol
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // 2️⃣ Build map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        world: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://YOUR_BUCKET.s3.amazonaws.com/world.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'land',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'land',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#e5e5e5' }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Replace `https://YOUR_BUCKET.s3.amazonaws.com/world.pmtiles` with the public URL of your own archive.

---

## 8️⃣ Generating a `.pmtiles` file (quick tip)

If you already have an MBTiles file (e.g. from `tippecanoe` or `ogr2ogr`), convert it in one line:

```bash
# Download the CLI binary (or use Docker)
curl -L -o pmtiles https://github.com/protomaps/go-pmtiles/releases/download/v2.5.0/pmtiles-linux-amd64
chmod +x pmtiles

# Convert
./pmtiles convert mytiles.mbtiles mytiles.pmtiles
```

Now upload `mytiles.pmtiles` to your static host and reference it as shown above.

---

## TL;DR – The 5-step cheat sheet

1. `npm i pmtiles` (or load via CDN).  
2. `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', new pmtiles.Protocol().tile);` (run once).  
3. In the style, use a **vector/raster/raster-dem** source with `url: "pmtiles://https://…/mydata.pmtiles"`.  
4. Ensure the host serves **Range** and **CORS** headers.  
5. Create the map – the tiles appear automatically.

That’s everything you need to load a `.pmtiles` archive as a source in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping! 🚀

## What changed in MapLibre GL JS v4?

In v3 the **protocol handler** you passed to `maplibregl.addProtocol()` had the signature  

```js
(params, callback) => { … }
```

You called `callback(err, data)` when the tile was ready (or an error).  
The handler could also return an object with a `cancel()` method for aborting the request.

Starting with **v4** the API was modernised:

* The handler is now **promise-based** (or an `async` function).  
* It receives **`params`** and an **`AbortController`** (instead of a callback).  
* The function **must resolve to an object that contains a `data` property** (the raw tile bytes).  
  Returning a bare `ArrayBuffer`, `Blob`, `Response`, or `Uint8Array` is **invalid** – MapLibre will treat it as “no data” and you’ll see blank tiles.  
* The optional `cancel` method is replaced by the `AbortController` you get as the second argument.

Because of that, any code that still uses the old callback style will silently fail – the tile request resolves, but MapLibre never sees the data.

---

## Quick fix: rewrite the handler to the new contract

Below is a minimal “drop-in” replacement for a typical callback-style handler.

### Old v3 style (doesn’t work in v4)

```js
maplibregl.addProtocol('custom', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));

  // optional cancel token for the old API
  return { cancel: () => {} };
});
```

### New v4+ style (works)

```js
maplibregl.addProtocol('custom', async (params, abortController) => {
  // Abort the fetch if MapLibre cancels the request
  const response = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });

  if (!response.ok) {
    // Throwing aborts the promise and MapLibre reports an error for the tile
    throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
  }

  // Get the raw bytes (ArrayBuffer, Uint8Array, Blob, etc.)
  const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // IMPORTANT: wrap the bytes in an object with a `data` key
  return { data: buffer };
});
```

**Key points**

| v3 | v4 |
|----|----|
| `(params, callback)` | `(params, abortController)` |
| Call `callback(err, data)` | `throw` on error, `return {data}` on success |
| Optional `{ cancel() { … } }` | Use `abortController.signal` – no explicit cancel object needed |
| Return value ignored | Must be a **Promise** (or `async` function) that resolves to `{data, cacheControl?, expires?}` |

---

## Full example with a real-world use-case (PMTiles)

If you are only trying to use **PMTiles**, you don’t need to write a handler at all – the library already ships a compatible one that works on every MapLibre version:

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Create the protocol object once (e.g. in your app entry point)
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it – the `tile` method is a v4-compatible shim
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);
```

That single line replaces any custom callback handler you may have written for PMTiles. It automatically:

* Handles the `AbortController` logic,
* Returns `{data}`,
* Caches tiles according to HTTP `Cache-Control`/`Expires`,
* Works with both vector and raster PMTiles.

If you *do* need a custom protocol (e.g. you are serving tiles from a proprietary API), use the async pattern shown above.

---

## Step-by-step migration checklist

1. **Locate every `addProtocol` call** in your codebase.  
2. Replace the callback signature with an `async` function that receives `(params, abortController)`.  
3. Inside the function:
   * Use `fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal })` (or any other XHR/fetch you prefer).  
   * Throw on non-OK responses.  
   * Convert the response to an `ArrayBuffer` (or `Blob`, `Uint8Array`, etc.).  
   * **Return `{ data: <your-bytes> }`.**  
4. Remove any `return { cancel: … }` objects – they are no longer consulted.  
5. If you need to expose cache hints, you can also return `cacheControl` and `expires`:

   ```js
   return {
     data: buffer,
     cacheControl: response.headers.get('Cache-Control'), // optional
     expires: response.headers.get('Expires')               // optional
   };
   ```

6. **Register the protocol before any `new maplibregl.Map()`** is called. The registry is global, so a single registration works for all maps on the page.  
7. Test a few tiles in the network tab:
   * You should see a **Range** request (`bytes=…`) to your tile source.  
   * The response body size should match the tile size (e.g. ~10 KB for a vector tile).  
   * No “empty tile” or “0 bytes” responses.  
8. If you are using **React** or another framework, move the registration to a top-level effect (e.g. `useEffect(() => { … }, [])`) so it runs once, not on every component render.

---

## Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Tiles appear blank (vector) or white (raster) but network shows a 200 response | Handler returned a raw `ArrayBuffer` instead of `{data: buffer}` | Wrap the buffer in `{data: buffer}` |
| Console shows `Error: Tile fetch error: …` after upgrade | You are still using the callback style (MapLibre never calls it) | Convert to async handler as shown |
| Requests are never sent (no network activity) | Protocol was registered **after** the map was created | Register the protocol **before** `new maplibregl.Map()` |
| Range requests are missing `Accept-Ranges: bytes` in the response | Server (S3, R2, etc.) not configured for range requests or CORS | Enable `Range` support and `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain) |
| You get “AbortError: The user aborted a request.” for every tile | Your handler is ignoring the `abortController` and the fetch is being aborted immediately (e.g., you called `abortController.abort()` yourself) | Do **not** call `abort()`; just pass the signal to `fetch` |
| Using `tiles: ['pmtiles://…/{z}/{x}/{y}']` and the map keeps requesting zoom levels that don’t exist | `tiles` bypasses the PMTiles header that contains `minzoom`/`maxzoom` | Use `url: 'pmtiles://https://…/myfile.pmtiles'` instead of `tiles:` |

---

## Full working example (custom protocol + PMTiles fallback)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre v4 – custom protocol migration</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Register the built-in PMTiles handler (recommended)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const pmProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmProtocol.tile);

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ (Optional) Register a *custom* protocol that
  //    fetches tiles from a private API.
  // -------------------------------------------------
  maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abortController) => {
    // Example: params.url === "mytiles://https://api.example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}"
    // Strip the custom scheme before fetching
    const url = params.url.replace(/^mytiles:\/\//, '');

    const resp = await fetch(url, { signal: abortController.signal });
    if (!resp.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Tile error ${resp.status}`);
    }
    const buf = await resp.arrayBuffer();
    return { data: buf };
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Create the map – note the source uses the `pmtiles` scheme
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Vector PMTiles example
        osm: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/monaco.pmtiles'
        },

        // Custom-protocol raster example (if you need it)
        // myRaster: {
        //   type: 'raster',
        //   url: 'mytiles://https://api.example.com/raster/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
        // }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'osm',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'osm',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [7.4246, 43.7384], // Monaco
    zoom: 13
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ Clean-up (optional but good practice)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('remove', () => {
    maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
    maplibregl.removeProtocol('mytiles');
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

* The **PMTiles** source works out-of-the-box because `pmtilesProtocol.tile` already follows the v4 contract.  
* The **custom** `mytiles` protocol shows the exact shape you need for any other tile service.

---

## TL;DR – What you need to change

```js
// ❌ v3 style (callback)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
  return { cancel: () => {} };
});

// ✅ v4+ style (async, returns {data})
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  const resp = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });
  if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`Tile error ${resp.status}`);
  const buf = await resp.arrayBuffer();
  return { data: buf };
});
```

If you are only using PMTiles, replace your whole custom handler with:

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', new pmtiles.Protocol().tile);
```

That’s all you need to get your map working again after the v3→v4 upgrade. Happy mapping!

**Short answer:** No – PMTiles is a *static* archive of pre-generated tiles. It’s perfect for basemaps, historic datasets, or any data that doesn’t change between deployments, but it can’t serve live bus positions that update every 30 seconds from PostGIS.

Below is a quick decision matrix, the reasons why PMTiles isn’t a fit for this use-case, and a recommended architecture that still lets you keep a static PMTiles basemap while overlaying the real-time bus data.

---

## 1️⃣ Decision matrix (static vs. dynamic)

| Requirement | Static tile archive (PMTiles) | Dynamic tile service / GeoJSON |
|-------------|------------------------------|--------------------------------|
| **Data changes frequently** (seconds-to-minutes) | ❌ Cannot update without re-creating the whole file (or a new file) and re-uploading it. | ✅ Serve fresh data on each request (e.g., via a tile server, vector-tile endpoint, or plain GeoJSON). |
| **Zero-ops hosting** (S3, GitHub Pages, CDN) | ✅ Upload one file, set cache headers, done. | ❌ You need a server (or serverless function) that can query PostGIS and return data. |
| **Low latency for live positions** | ❌ Even if you regenerated the archive every minute, the client would still have to download a new *.pmtiles* file (tens-to-hundreds MB). | ✅ Only the changed features are sent (few KB per request). |
| **Client-side styling flexibility** | ✅ All vector layers are baked in; you can style them freely. | ✅ You can still style GeoJSON or vector-tile layers, but you need to expose the source. |
| **Cache friendliness** | ✅ CDN caches the whole file; range requests are cheap. | ✅ Tile-level caching works, but you must configure appropriate `Cache-Control` / `ETag` for dynamic endpoints. |

**Bottom line:** Use PMTiles for the *basemap* (streets, landcover, terrain, etc.) and a *dynamic* source for the bus positions.

---

## 2️⃣ Why PMTiles won’t work for live bus data

1. **Immutable archive** – Once a *.pmtiles* file is written, its contents are fixed. Updating a single feature (a bus) would require rewriting the whole archive (or at least the affected tile groups) and uploading a new file. That’s far too heavyweight for a 30-second update cycle.

2. **No server-side query** – PMTiles is just a file; the client can only request byte ranges that correspond to pre-computed tiles. It can’t ask “give me all buses within this bounding box right now”.

3. **Cache-busting nightmare** – Even if you regenerated the file every 30 s, browsers/CDNs would cache the old version unless you change the URL (e.g., add a query string). That defeats the whole point of a static, cache-friendly asset.

4. **File size** – A country-scale PMTiles archive is often tens to hundreds of megabytes. Re-downloading that every half-minute would be wasteful and would quickly hit bandwidth limits.

---

## 3️⃣ Recommended architecture

### 3.1 Static basemap (PMTiles)

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Register the protocol once (e.g. in your app entry point)
const pmProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmProtocol.tile);

// Create the map with a PMTiles basemap source
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      basemap: {
        type: 'vector',
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/europe-basemap.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // …your usual street, water, landcover layers that reference `basemap`
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 5
});
```

*Result:* A fast, CDN-cached basemap that never needs a server.

### 3.2 Dynamic bus positions (GeoJSON or vector-tile endpoint)

#### Option A – **GeoJSON source with periodic fetch**

```js
// 1️⃣ Add a GeoJSON source (empty at start)
map.addSource('buses', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: { type: 'FeatureCollection', features: [] }
});

// 2️⃣ Add a layer to render the points
map.addLayer({
  id: 'bus-points',
  type: 'circle',
  source: 'buses',
  paint: {
    'circle-radius': 6,
    'circle-color': '#ff6600',
    'circle-stroke-width': 1,
    'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
  }
});

// 3️⃣ Poll the server every 30 s
async function refreshBuses() {
  try {
    const resp = await fetch('/api/bus-positions'); // returns GeoJSON
    if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${resp.status}`);
    const geojson = await resp.json();
    map.getSource('buses').setData(geojson);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Failed to fetch bus positions', e);
  }
}
refreshBuses();               // initial load
setInterval(refreshBuses, 30_000);
```

*Server side:* A tiny HTTP endpoint (Node/Express, FastAPI, Go, etc.) that runs a `SELECT` against PostGIS and returns a FeatureCollection. No tile server needed; just a JSON API.

#### Option B – **Vector-tile endpoint (e.g., Martin, Tileserver-GL, or a custom TileJSON service)**

If you have many buses (hundreds-thousands) or want to benefit from tile-level caching, expose a **vector-tile endpoint** that reads from PostGIS on each request:

```
GET https://my-api.example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
```

- **Martin** (`martin` from maplibre.org) can read directly from a PostGIS table and serve vector tiles without pre-generating them.
- **Tileserver-GL** can be configured with a *PostGIS* source via the `postgis` plugin.
- Or write a tiny serverless function (e.g., Cloudflare Workers, AWS Lambda) that runs a `ST_AsMVT` query and returns the MVT.

Then in the style:

```json
{
  "id": "buses",
  "type": "vector",
  "url": "pmtiles://https://my-api.example.com/bus-tiles.json"
}
```

*Why you might pick this:*  
- Tile-level caching reduces bandwidth when the map is zoomed out (many buses are aggregated into a single tile).  
- You can still keep the 30-second refresh interval by setting `Cache-Control: max-age=30` on the tile responses.

### 3.3 Putting it together

```js
// Basemap (static PMTiles) – already added above
// Dynamic bus source (choose A or B)

// Example using the GeoJSON approach:
map.on('load', () => {
  // add the dynamic source & layer here (see code above)
});
```

**Result:**  
- **Basemap** loads instantly from a CDN, no server required.  
- **Bus positions** update every 30 s from a lightweight API that queries PostGIS.  
- You keep the operational simplicity of static hosting while still delivering real-time data.

---

## 4️⃣ Quick checklist before you start

| ✅ Item | Why it matters |
|--------|----------------|
| **Static basemap in PMTiles** | One file, CDN-cached, no server. |
| **CORS & Range support** on the host (S3, CloudFront, R2, etc.) | MapLibre must be able to request byte ranges. |
| **Dynamic endpoint** (GeoJSON or vector tiles) | Serves fresh bus positions. |
| **Cache-Control** on the dynamic endpoint (`max-age=30` or `no-cache`) | Guarantees the browser asks for fresh data each poll. |
| **Authentication / rate-limiting** (if the bus data is private) | Use signed URLs, JWT, or API keys on the dynamic endpoint. |
| **Separate concerns** – keep the PMTiles file immutable; never rewrite it on each bus update. | Prevents huge bandwidth waste and cache-busting headaches. |

---

## 5️⃣ TL;DR

- **Do not use PMTiles for live bus positions.** They are static archives and cannot be updated every 30 seconds without re-uploading a huge file.
- **Use PMTiles for the static basemap** (streets, terrain, etc.) – this gives you a zero-ops, CDN-friendly background.
- **Serve bus positions via a dynamic source**: a simple GeoJSON API that you poll, or a vector-tile endpoint (Martin, Tileserver-GL, custom serverless function) that queries PostGIS on demand.
- Combine the two in a single MapLibre style, and you’ll have a fast, low-maintenance map that still shows real-time bus locations.

If you need help wiring the dynamic endpoint (e.g., a minimal Express route that returns GeoJSON from PostGIS) or configuring a vector-tile server like Martin, just let me know – I can give you a concrete code snippet. Happy mapping!
